2026-06-30T16:24:43.456Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sunquan/ai_coding/CODING/rick/internal/cmd/learning.go","content":"// buildLearningPrompt builds learning prompt and saves to promptsDir/learning_prompt.md.\n// Returns the prompt file path; all files are persistent, no cleanup needed.\nfunc buildLearningPrompt(data *ExecutionData, learningDir, promptsDir string) (string, error) {\n\tpromptMgr := prompt.NewPromptManager(\"\")\n\n\ttemplate, err := promptMgr.LoadTemplate(\"learning\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"failed to load learning template: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\n\tbuilder := prompt.NewPromptBuilder(template)\n\n\tbuilder.SetVariable(\"job_id\", data.JobID)\n\tbuilder.SetVariable(\"learning_dir\", learningDir)\n\n\t// OKR — full content\n\tif data.OKRContent != \"\" {\n\t\tbuilder.SetVariable(\"okr_content\", data.OKRContent)\n\t} else {\n\t\tbuilder.SetVariable(\"okr_content\", \"（本 job 无 OKR.md）\")\n\t}\n\n\trickDir := filepath.Dir(data.SpecPath) // .rick/SPEC.md → .rick/\n\tloopsDir := filepath.Join(rickDir, \"loops\")\n\tskillsDir := filepath.Join(rickDir, \"skills\")\n\tbuilder.SetVariable(\"loops_dir\", loopsDir)\n\tbuilder.SetVariable(\"skills_dir\", skillsDir)\n\tbuilder.SetVariable(\"loops_context\", prompt.LoadLoopsContext(loopsDir))\n\n\t// debug.md — embed content directly\n\tif data.DebugContent != \"\" {\n\t\tbuilder.SetVariable(\"debug_content\", data.DebugContent)\n\t} else {\n\t\tbuilder.SetVariable(\"debug_content\", \"（本次 job 无 debug.md 记录）\")\n\t}\n\n\t// task*.md paths\n\tif len(data.TaskMDPaths) > 0 {\n\t\tvar sb strings.Builder\n\t\tfor _, p := range data.TaskMDPaths {","numLines":40,"startLine":272,"totalLines":364}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_vrtx_01DxLE1BK9e8oAAKdSGMRYcF","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-4-7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the issue clearly. In `runLearningDryRun`, `data.SpecPath` is set to `filepath.Join(rickDir, \"SPEC.md\")`, and then inside `buildLearningPrompt`, `rickDir` is derived from `data.SpecPath` as `filepath.Dir(data.SpecPath)`.\n\nSo `loopsDir` = `/path/to/.rick/loops` and `skillsDir` = `/path/to/.rick/skills`. These are correctly resolved.\n\nThe issue is just that the test checks for `\"loops_dir\"` (the string) in the output, but after variable resolution, only the actual paths appear (not the string \"loops_dir\").\nThe simplest fix would be to add debug output in `runLearningDryRun` that prints the variable names alongside their resolved paths, but the problem is that `loopsDir` and `skillsDir` are computed inside `buildLearningPrompt`, not in the outer function where I'd want to print them.\n\nI could either compute those paths in `runLearningDryRun` itself and print them before calling the build function, or I could take the less invasive approach of updating the test to check for path substrings like `.rick/loops` and `.rick/skills` instead of expecting the variable names to appear in the output. Option 2 seems cleaner since it doesn't require restructuring the code. I need to reconsider what the spec is actually asking for. The requirement mentions \"loops_dir\" and \"skills_dir\" paths in the output, but the actual paths are `/path/.rick/loops` and `/path/.rick/skills`. Looking at the test, it's checking whether the literal strings \"loops_dir\" and \"skills_dir\" appear in the dry-run output, which means I should include a debug header that explicitly labels these variables rather than just printing the raw paths.","signature":""}],"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use